ext{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3t-5+2{t}^{-1}) \\\\  & =3+2(-{t}^{-2}) \\\\  & =3-\\frac{2}{{t}^{2}}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534090 true] [17592186277260 1000 #edb/ref 17592186277261 13194139534090 true] [17592186277260 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139534090 true] [17592186277260 1041 "field-1" 13194139534090 true] [17592186277260 1104 #edb/ref 17592186277261 13194139534090 true] [17592186277260 1110 #uuid "7452ce5f-f87e-5225-8bf5-cca2914bd318" 13194139534090 true] [17592186277261 1034 "3-\\frac{2}{t^2}" 13194139534090 true] [17592186277261 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139534090 true] [17592186277261 1111 #uuid "0c20258f-2a0b-536b-9bad-91fa23c21b8b" 13194139534090 true] [17592186277262 1017 #edb/ref 17592186277263 13194139534090 true] [17592186277262 1089 "q-336441" 13194139534090 true] [17592186277262 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534090 true] [17592186277262 1100 "The displacement (in meters) of a particle moving in a straight line is given by $s(t)=4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2},$ where $t$ is measured in seconds. Find the time when the particle's acceleration is equal to $a=70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$\n\nThe acceleration is $70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ at\n$t=$\n{{field-1}} seconds." 13194139534090 true] [17592186277262 1113 #uuid "565b31b4-2cd1-5b24-8e88-4ac0f4ecf026" 13194139534090 true] [17592186277262 1145 "First, we calculate $v(t)$ by differentiating $s(t):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}s}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4{t}^{3}-{t}^{2}) \\\\  & =12{t}^{2}-2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we differentiate $v(t)$ to find $a(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(t) & =\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(12{t}^{2}-2t) \\\\  & =24t-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo calculate the time when the acceleration is $a=70,$ we substitute this into the above:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}70 & =24t-2 \\\\ 24t & =72 \\\\ t & =3\\text{ }\\text{s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the acceleration is $70\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}$ at $t=3$ seconds